---
<MainLayout title="West Covina November 17 Council Agenda: Traffic Project, Overtime and Oversight | StayJustIn" description="StayJustIn examines West Covina's November 17, 2026 Council agenda: a $5.26 million traffic project, apparent overtime reporting errors, budget changes, and police equipment oversight." canonical="https://www.stayjustin.com/city-hall/west-covina-november-17-2026-agenda-preview/">
  <article class="article-page">
    <div class="container article-container council-preview">
      <p class="page-kicker">WEST COVINA CITY HALL · UPDATED NOVEMBER 17, 2026 · READ BEFORE TONIGHT’S 7 PM MEETING</p>
      <h1>Before Tonight's West Covina Council Meeting: Fire Overtime Nears Its Annual Budget, a $5.3M Traffic Plan, and Contract Questions</h1>
      <p class="article-dek"><strong>Before tonight’s 7 p.m. Council meeting: seven consent-calendar items, a questionable overtime table, a major traffic project, and unanswered questions about contract oversight.</strong></p>
      <p><em>By StayJustIn · {date} · Pre-meeting agenda analysis</em></p>
      <p>A $359,917 engineering agreement. A $5.26 million traffic-safety program. A first-quarter financial report showing millions more spent than received so far. These are the kinds of details that can disappear into a routine agenda summary unless residents read the attachments.</p>
      <p>None of the issues identified here establishes wrongdoing. The purpose of this preview is to distinguish what the City's reports say from what still needs verification.</p>

      <p>Tonight's agenda deserves more than a glance at the dollar amounts. The City's first-quarter financial report, engineering consultant selection, and police equipment report each contain details residents should understand before the Council votes.</p>
      <p>One issue stands out: reported Fire Department overtime spending appears to equal about <strong>95% of its annual budget after only three months</strong>, although the published table displays 40%. The difference needs clarification; it does not, by itself, prove misuse of funds.</p>
      <div class="chart-block" role="img" aria-label="Fire overtime: 1,115,402 dollars spent of 1,174,567 dollars budgeted, about 95 percent">
        <h3>Fire overtime: nearly the entire annual allocation in three months</h3>
        <div class="bar-row"><span>Fire Department</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:95%"></div></div><strong>95%</strong></div>
        <small>$1,115,402 reported through September 30 against $1,174,567 budgeted for the year. The City's published percentage shows 40%; the figures need reconciliation.</small>
      </div>
      <h2>The $21.9 million first-quarter question</h2>
      <p>The City's first-quarter General Fund report lists approximately <strong>$5.9 million in revenue</strong> and <strong>$27.8 million in expenditures</strong> from July 1 through September 30, 2026 — a difference of about <strong>$21.9 million</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>That is not evidence of a $21.9 million annual deficit.</strong> Property-tax distributions and sales-tax receipts arrive unevenly, so first-quarter receipts do not represent one-quarter of the City's expected annual revenue.</p>
      <p>The adopted General Fund budget anticipates approximately $99.8 million in annual revenue and $99.7 million in expenditures, leaving a projected positive margin of about $108,000. That is roughly 0.1% of projected revenue. The important question is whether the full-year forecast still holds after the first quarter.</p>

      <h2>The overtime table that doesn't add up</h2>
      <p>The financial report presents annual overtime budgets alongside first-quarter spending, but some displayed percentages do not match the corresponding amounts.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Reported overtime figures and calculated percentages</caption><thead><tr><th>Department</th><th>Annual budget</th><th>Reported spending</th><th>Displayed</th><th>Calculated</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Fire</td><td>$1,174,567</td><td>$1,115,402</td><td>40%</td><td><strong>95.0%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Police</td><td>$1,784,674</td><td>$714,426</td><td>75.8%</td><td><strong>40.0%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>General Fund total</td><td>~$3.12M</td><td>~$1.87M</td><td>—</td><td><strong>~59.8%</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p><strong>What the discrepancy means:</strong> If the dollar figures are correct, the Fire Department has used approximately 95% of its listed annual overtime allocation in the first quarter. If the percentages are correct, some dollar amounts or row labels need correction. A formatting or placement error is one possible explanation; the City has not yet provided an explanation in the materials analyzed.</p>
      <p>Overtime may reflect staffing levels, emergencies, or other operational needs. The mismatch alone does not establish improper spending, but the underlying accounting schedule should be reconciled before residents rely on the table.</p>

      <h2>Approximately $2.51 million in proposed budget adjustments</h2>
      <p>The first-quarter report also proposes additional appropriations across multiple <strong>non-General Fund</strong> accounts. They should not be characterized as interchangeable unrestricted taxpayer funds. Items described in the packet include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>$627,000</strong> for stormwater maintenance, permits, and compliance.</li>
        <li><strong>$200,000</strong> for additional landscaping in Maintenance District No. 1.</li>
        <li><strong>$182,299</strong> in Community Services Foundation funds associated with a wellness fitness center.</li>
        <li><strong>$130,805</strong> for information-technology software licensing.</li>
        <li><strong>$100,000</strong> for BKK-site maintenance.</li>
        <li><strong>$35,422</strong> for a Police Department replacement motorcycle, described as reimbursable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The report also describes $1,222,625 in Measure A revenue and corresponding spending for Cameron Avenue and Cameron Park projects. The key questions are whether each expenditure is required, what it buys, and whether it creates future recurring costs.</p>

      <h2>The $5.26 million traffic-safety project</h2>
      <p>The City proposes a <strong>$5,259,100</strong> traffic-safety program affecting <strong>64 signalized intersections</strong>. Staff describes improvements to signal equipment, visibility, pedestrian safety, and protected left-turn phases at four locations:</p>
      <ol><li>Glendora Avenue and Merced Avenue</li><li>Cameron Avenue and Lark Ellen Avenue</li><li>Westport Street and Amar Road</li><li>Cameron Avenue and Valinda Avenue</li></ol>
      <div class="chart-block" role="img" aria-label="Proposed traffic project funding: federal 4,733,190 dollars, 90 percent; local Measure M 525,910 dollars, 10 percent">
        <h3>Proposed $5.26 million project funding</h3>
        <div class="funding-bar"><div class="federal"></div><div class="local"></div></div>
        <div class="funding-legend"><span><i class="swatch federal-swatch"></i> Federal: <strong>$4,733,190 · 90%</strong></span><span><i class="swatch local-swatch"></i> Local Measure M: <strong>$525,910 · 10%</strong></span></div>
      </div>
      <p><strong>The Council is not being asked to award a $5.26 million construction contract at this meeting.</strong> The immediate recommendation is a <strong>$359,917.83 preliminary-engineering agreement</strong> with Kimley-Horn and Associates, Inc. Engineering is expected to start in January 2027, with the construction bid package anticipated in late 2028.</p>

      <h2>Kimley-Horn's earlier West Covina work deserves a closer look</h2>
      <p>Kimley-Horn is not a new name in the City's engineering work. According to the proposal details reviewed for this story, the firm identifies four other West Covina projects involving traffic signals, pedestrian safety, and Azusa Avenue.</p>
      <p>Records described in this investigation also identify a <strong>July 2025 Council-approved engineering agreement of approximately $544,000</strong>, with about <strong>$90,000 in optional work</strong>. During that meeting, councilmembers reportedly questioned the use of Measure M funds alongside approximately $7 million in federal funding discussed for the community; staff said it was working with Caltrans to clarify requirements.</p>
      <p><strong>What remains unverified:</strong> StayJustIn has not independently confirmed whether the federal funding was ultimately used for that earlier work, whether the optional services were authorized, or whether the earlier contract amount changed. These are questions for the City's contract records, not evidence of improper contracting.</p>
      <h2>Kimley-Horn won by 0.34 points</h2>
      <p>Six firms submitted engineering proposals. A three-person panel scored qualifications, understanding, experience, and schedule. Kimley-Horn's average score was <strong>87.67</strong>; General Technologies and Solutions scored <strong>87.33</strong>.</p>
      <div class="chart-block" role="img" aria-label="Engineering proposal scores: Kimley-Horn 87.67, General Technologies and Solutions 87.33, difference 0.34 points">
        <h3>Engineering proposal evaluation scores</h3>
        <div class="bar-row"><span>Kimley-Horn</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:87.67%"></div></div><strong>87.67</strong></div>
        <div class="bar-row"><span>General Technologies</span><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:87.33%"></div></div><strong>87.33</strong></div>
        <small>Scores out of 100; the difference is 0.34 points. Only the two highest scores are shown here.</small>
      </div>
      <p>Price was excluded from the initial scoring under the qualifications-based federal-aid selection procedure described by staff. A narrow margin does not invalidate the award. It does make the individual scoring sheets and negotiated fee analysis useful records for public review.</p>
      <p>Staff also proposes allowing the City Manager to execute future contract amendments. The proposed authorization is especially worth examining given the firm’s previous City work. The initial agreement contains a compensation limit and requires written approval for added services, but the agenda recommendation does not identify a separate dollar ceiling for future amendments. Residents should ask what purchasing rules govern those changes and when an amendment must return to the Council.</p>

      <h2>Audit Committee: what has actually been corrected?</h2>
      <p>The Council is receiving an informational summary of 2026 Audit Committee meetings covering purchasing rules, a purchasing-policy violation log, financial statements, and corrective action planning related to the California State Auditor's findings.</p>
      <p>A committee discussing an issue is not the same as a policy being implemented or independently verified. StayJustIn's <a href="/accountability/west-covina-high-risk-audit/">separate State Auditor analysis</a> examines the difference between the auditor's last documented assessment and the City's newer draft corrective action plan.</p>

      <h2>Police drones and military equipment reporting</h2>
      <p>Under California's AB 481 reporting framework, the Police Department's annual military equipment report covers a broad range of equipment, including drones, armored vehicles, specialized firearms, and other tools.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Deployments and expenditures as reported by the Police Department</caption><thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Reported activity</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Drones</td><td>97 field deployments</td></tr><tr><td>BearCat armored vehicle</td><td>5 deployments</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile command post</td><td>12 deployments</td></tr><tr><td>Flashbang devices</td><td>5 deployments</td></tr><tr><td>Drone-related spending</td><td>$59,940.24 for fleet replacement</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>The department reports no complaints, concerns, or internally identified policy violations. These are <strong>department-reported conclusions</strong>, not independent findings by StayJustIn. The document is identified as a 2025 report, but some table labels reference 2024; the City should clarify the reporting period.</p>
      <p><strong>Public meeting:</strong> The department lists a community engagement meeting for <strong>November 24, 2026, at 5 p.m.</strong> in the City Council Chambers. Residents can ask about equipment uses, spending, and oversight.</p>

      <h2>Other items on the agenda</h2>
      <p>Other business includes a proposal to cancel the December 1 regular Council meeting and hold a special meeting on <strong>December 3 at 7 p.m.</strong>, pay-schedule changes tied to California's January 2027 minimum wage of <strong>$17.40 per hour</strong>, and approval of the October 20 meeting minutes. Staff says the wage adjustments can be absorbed within the existing budget.</p>

      <h2>Tonight: what residents can ask the Council</h2>
      <ol>{questions.map((question) => <li>{question}</li>)}</ol>
      <p>Consent-calendar items may be approved together without individual discussion unless an item is pulled. Residents can raise questions through the meeting's public-comment process, subject to the posted agenda and Council procedures.</p>
      <p><strong>Meeting reminder:</strong> Tuesday, November 17, 2026, at <strong>7 p.m. at West Covina City Hall</strong>. All seven items described here appear on the consent calendar. Councilmembers can request separate discussion of individual items; residents should consult the posted public-comment rules for participation.</p>
      <p><strong>Question for neighbors:</strong> Should the Council pull the financial report and Kimley-Horn contract for separate public discussion, or is the available documentation sufficient? Both perspectives deserve to be evaluated against the same records.</p>
      <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> A large contract is not automatically a bad contract. A reporting error is not proof of misconduct. But residents should expect accurate tables, documented evaluations, defined spending authority, and clear evidence of promised reforms.</p>
      <p><strong>Stay informed. Stay involved. StayJustIn.</strong></p>

      <aside class="verification-note">
        <h2>Reporting notes and verification limits</h2>
        <p>This is a <strong>pre-meeting preview</strong>, not a report of actions taken November 17. It draws on the agenda details and figures supplied for this analysis. The full individual engineering score sheets, competing fee proposals, the cited July 2025 agreement and related Council discussion, historical contract amendments, underlying overtime accounting schedule, and current purchasing violation log have not been independently re-examined for this update. The City has not yet explained the apparent percentage mismatches. The article will be updated if those records or corrections become available.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
